1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,210
This is a swing ride. It's
inside a cooling tower.

2
00:00:03,230 --> 00:00:06,230
And the cooling tower
belongs to a nuclear power

3
00:00:06,250 --> 00:00:10,550
station that cost West Germany
around 7 billion marks.

4
00:00:10,930 --> 00:00:16,210
It was finished in 1985. It was
kept hot and ready for six years.

5
00:00:16,230 --> 00:00:18,670
And it never made a single watt.

6
00:00:19,070 --> 00:00:23,050
This is the story of a farmer
who fought it for 13 years,

7
00:00:23,310 --> 00:00:25,630
a state that wouldn't
sign the last permit,

8
00:00:25,650 --> 00:00:28,930
and the Dutchman who
ended up owning the lot.

9
00:00:31,370 --> 00:00:36,210
In the 1960s, West Germany wanted a
reactor that could make its own fuel.

10
00:00:36,670 --> 00:00:38,390
It's called a fast breeder.

11
00:00:39,110 --> 00:00:44,250
As it runs, it turns ordinary
uranium into new plutonium fuel.

12
00:00:44,730 --> 00:00:47,750
In theory, it makes
more fuel than it burns.

13
00:00:48,450 --> 00:00:52,050
Germany, Belgium and the
Netherlands went into it together.

14
00:00:52,490 --> 00:00:57,170
The site they chose was Kalkar,
a small town on the Lower Rhine.

15
00:00:57,770 --> 00:01:00,770
One reason, according to
the project's own history:

16
00:01:00,950 --> 00:01:02,850
it was close to the Dutch partner.

17
00:01:03,190 --> 00:01:04,810
You could reach it by bike.

18
00:01:05,413 --> 00:01:09,770
Building started in April 1973,
in the middle of farmland.

19
00:01:10,800 --> 00:01:13,200
The price was the first thing to go.

20
00:01:13,440 --> 00:01:18,300
In 1965, the estimate was 310 million marks.

21
00:01:18,640 --> 00:01:21,900
By 1971, 670 million.

22
00:01:22,460 --> 00:01:26,900
The contract signed in 1972
said 1.5 billion.

23
00:01:27,480 --> 00:01:30,300
Ten years later, it was more than 6 billion.

24
00:01:30,750 --> 00:01:34,270
In the end, it came to
around 7 billion marks.

25
00:01:35,270 --> 00:01:39,030
Right next to the site lived
a farmer called Josef Maas.

26
00:01:39,370 --> 00:01:41,890
His family had farmed
there for generations.

27
00:01:42,550 --> 00:01:47,630
In 1972, before construction had
even started, he went to court.

28
00:01:48,090 --> 00:01:51,610
His case went all the way to
Germany's Constitutional Court.

29
00:01:52,250 --> 00:01:55,870
In 1978, the court said
the breeder was legal.

30
00:01:56,410 --> 00:01:59,230
But it also said that a decision this big,

31
00:01:59,450 --> 00:02:03,410
whether the country should use nuclear
power at all, belongs to Parliament.

32
00:02:04,330 --> 00:02:07,890
German law students still learn
it as the Kalkar decision.

33
00:02:08,470 --> 00:02:11,010
Outside the courts, the fight got bigger.

34
00:02:11,490 --> 00:02:17,090
In September 1977, tens of thousands
of people marched on Kalkar.

35
00:02:17,110 --> 00:02:21,310
Police set up checkpoints on the roads
in, including on the Rhine bridge.

36
00:02:21,890 --> 00:02:24,850
And around the reactor, a wall went up.

37
00:02:25,570 --> 00:02:27,870
But the real obstacle
wasn't the protesters.

38
00:02:28,330 --> 00:02:33,590
It was paperwork. Kalkar was
licensed in stages, permit by permit,

39
00:02:33,610 --> 00:02:36,008
by the state of North Rhine-Westphalia.

40
00:02:36,570 --> 00:02:41,410
In 1984, there was a sodium fire on
the roof of the reactor building.

41
00:02:41,914 --> 00:02:44,590
The next year, the
state's politics turned.

42
00:02:45,370 --> 00:02:47,870
Friedhelm Farthmann had
been the state minister

43
00:02:47,890 --> 00:02:51,210
responsible for about a
dozen of Kalkar's permits.

44
00:02:51,770 --> 00:02:55,170
In 1985, he gave an
interview to Der Spiegel.

45
00:02:55,790 --> 00:02:59,329
The headline was: "Don't
kindle this hell fire."

46
00:02:59,610 --> 00:03:01,050
Then came Chernobyl.

47
00:03:01,930 --> 00:03:06,230
The state and the federal government ended
up in the Constitutional Court again,

48
00:03:06,550 --> 00:03:09,530
and in 1990 the federal government won.

49
00:03:10,090 --> 00:03:12,710
But the state never signed
the last two permits:

50
00:03:12,810 --> 00:03:16,470
one to bring in the fuel, and
one to switch the reactor on.

51
00:03:17,330 --> 00:03:19,410
Meanwhile, the plant was finished.

52
00:03:19,970 --> 00:03:24,570
In May 1985, its cooling system
was filled with liquid sodium,

53
00:03:24,690 --> 00:03:27,730
the metal that carries the
heat out of a fast breeder.

54
00:03:28,150 --> 00:03:32,710
Letting the sodium freeze, or draining
it, risked damaging the plant.

55
00:03:33,190 --> 00:03:36,010
So for six years, hot
sodium was kept moving

56
00:03:36,030 --> 00:03:39,030
through a reactor that had
never been switched on.

57
00:03:39,430 --> 00:03:45,170
From 1988, just keeping it ready
cost 105 million marks a year.

58
00:03:45,530 --> 00:03:47,210
The fuel was waiting too.

59
00:03:48,470 --> 00:03:51,770
205 plutonium fuel elements
had been made for it.

60
00:03:51,990 --> 00:03:56,930
123 sat in a store at
Hanau, and 82 in Belgium.

61
00:03:57,190 --> 00:03:59,250
Not one was ever loaded.

62
00:04:00,230 --> 00:04:03,850
On the 20th of March
1991, the federal research

63
00:04:03,870 --> 00:04:06,750
minister met the power
companies and Siemens.

64
00:04:07,230 --> 00:04:09,030
They agreed to stop paying.

65
00:04:09,650 --> 00:04:10,890
That was the end.

66
00:04:11,310 --> 00:04:14,850
The ministry's press release said
the responsibility for the end

67
00:04:14,870 --> 00:04:19,115
of Kalkar "clearly lies with the
State of North Rhine-Westphalia."

68
00:04:19,590 --> 00:04:22,810
The project's official history,
published by the research

69
00:04:22,830 --> 00:04:26,210
centre behind it, ends
with a line from Seneca:

70
00:04:26,630 --> 00:04:29,170
"The time will come when
our descendants will

71
00:04:29,190 --> 00:04:32,650
be amazed that we did not
know things so plain."

72
00:04:33,650 --> 00:04:35,050
Now go back to the farmer.

73
00:04:35,830 --> 00:04:42,630
By 1985, after 13 years in court, Josef
Maas was exhausted and deep in debt.

74
00:04:43,170 --> 00:04:45,250
About 1.5 million marks.

75
00:04:45,690 --> 00:04:47,670
So he sold the family farm.

76
00:04:47,890 --> 00:04:50,290
The buyer was the company
building the reactor,

77
00:04:50,310 --> 00:04:53,370
and the condition was
that he drop the lawsuits.

78
00:04:53,990 --> 00:04:58,850
The price, he later told a
newspaper, was 4.3 million marks.

79
00:04:59,410 --> 00:05:01,710
He moved 300 kilometres away.

80
00:05:02,170 --> 00:05:05,710
In 1995, the reactor itself was sold.

81
00:05:06,250 --> 00:05:11,610
The buyer was Hennie van der Most, a Dutch
businessman who builds amusement parks.

82
00:05:12,150 --> 00:05:14,530
From the country you could reach by bike.

83
00:05:14,950 --> 00:05:17,510
The price was reported as a few million.

84
00:05:17,930 --> 00:05:21,450
By Maas's own account, the
Dutchman paid less for the whole

85
00:05:21,470 --> 00:05:25,210
reactor than the reactor's
builders had paid for his farm.

86
00:05:25,650 --> 00:05:29,910
Within a few years, it was open:
"Nuclear Water Wonderland".

87
00:05:30,730 --> 00:05:35,830
Today it's Wunderland Kalkar, with
hotels on the site, a climbing wall up

88
00:05:35,850 --> 00:05:42,130
the outside of the cooling tower, and,
since 2009, a swing ride inside it.

89
00:05:43,030 --> 00:05:44,610
Kalkar isn't the only one.

90
00:05:44,690 --> 00:05:47,750
In Austria, the Zwentendorf
plant was finished,

91
00:05:47,770 --> 00:05:51,970
and then, in 1978, the
country voted on it.

92
00:05:52,290 --> 00:05:56,070
50.5 percent said no. It never started.

93
00:05:56,530 --> 00:06:01,390
On Long Island, the Shoreham plant cost
billions and never ran commercially.

94
00:06:01,950 --> 00:06:04,495
It was sold to the state for $1.

95
00:06:05,470 --> 00:06:09,230
Seneca's line came true, just
not the way they meant it.

96
00:06:09,410 --> 00:06:11,810
People are amazed by Kalkar.

97
00:06:11,830 --> 00:06:13,490
They pay to ride the swings.

98
00:06:14,110 --> 00:06:15,730
This was UNBUILT.

99
00:06:16,070 --> 00:06:20,410
Every picture in this video is real,
and the sources are in the description.
